  "policy": "# Sustained peak risk for existing source-matched lexical negatives\n\nThe completed latent-command experiment improved consumed Jenny command hits24\u219226/27 but increased nearword false clips44\u219252/324; Joe remained0/72. This motivates discrimination work, not mining those development recordings into training. Original source-balanced BCE averages negative loss across the whole presentation, which can dilute a short high-score region. That is a plausible objective mismatch, not a demonstrated cause of observed false wakes.\n\nUse the exact original c161 source-balanced sampler, initialization, scalar25981parameter model, frozen BN, context309/stride3, seed726/lexical727, Adam1e-5 and20000final-only4090updates. No latent-command substitutions, auxiliary head, nonlinear head, BN adaptation or failed-final warm start. Inputs and source draws follow the original sampler. Eight existing source-matched bare Nick/Next/Mix/Muse negative presentations per batch change only their loss: replace time-mean negative BCE with20 times the maximum eligible five-output negative log complement of the arithmetic sigmoid mean. Five-output windows must be fully within valid negative steps; exclude feature padding. Other120 presentations retain original region-normalized BCE, including all positive and ambient examples. No threshold, margin, temperature, new coefficient or mining schedule is introduced.\n\nFor each selected negative, risk = max_window[-log(1-mean_5(sigmoid(z)))]. Implement its log complement as logmeanexp(logsigmoid(-z)) for numerical stability without probability clipping. The term is an absolute event surrogate, not a guarantee about quantized decoding, cooldown or rearm. It can overfocus on one window, lower target sensitivity or overfit existing source voices. All eight selected rows remain represented; no score-dependent source selection or filtering occurs.\n\nThis differs from historical paired phrase ranking and temporal-tail-risk over five predefined endpoint views: no paired ranking term; maximum is over all valid whole-stream windows, and the probability-mean negative-event risk replaces the whole-time mean on selected negatives. It is also distinct from the failed positive latent-support experiment and old fixed-endpoint arithmetic-mean BCE. These failures remain evidence, not erased baselines. No numerical consistency/gradient comparison admission, new TTS or human recording/listening prerequisite.\n\nPush implementation and exact execution lock before one training run. Preserve original training session/exit/report, one final builtinINT8 export and full372legacy/408Jenny/360Joe complete-waveform fixed.91796875/smoothing5/continuous81600prime replay. Reuse existing c161 baseline traces; preserve all failures and generated-boundary timing as descriptive rather than phonetic truth. No checkpoint/LR/seed/threshold/calibration rescue. Independent threshold selection and final qualification remain outstanding; consumed development is not acceptance and frozenv30 is retained.\n"
